          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a note says \"use additional code\", is that optional?",
          "answer": "The retrieved passages explain what the instruction does, but they do not say whether it is mandatory or optional, so I can't give you a firm answer from this material.\n\n**What the passages say it does:** In the ICD-10-CM conventions summary (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions), \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" work as a pair that sets the order of codes. The underlying condition is reported first, and the manifestation (the related condition) is reported after it. The same document says \"code also\" means two codes are needed but does not set their order.\n\n**Examples in the corpus:** In the diabetes summary (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11), some diabetes complications need a second code. For example, diabetic chronic kidney disease is paired with a code for the stage of kidney disease. The passage describes the additional code as the one that \"carries the detail.\" The asthma and COPD summaries (coding-asthma-j45, coding-copd-j44) say their categories \"carry an explicit instruction to add\" a tobacco-related code, and that this code accompanies most records.\n\n**What the passages don't say:** None of them states whether a \"use additional code\" note is always required or can be skipped. For that, check the official ICD-10-CM Official Guidelines for Coding and Reporting, or ask a certified medical coder or your coding department. If you share the exact note and the code it sits under, a coder can tell you how it applies in that case.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
